# Example env file for the weather-tracking compose project.
#
# Copy this to docker/weather.env (gitignored — real secrets never get
# committed) and fill in real values:
#   cp docker/weather.env.example docker/weather.env
#
# docker-compose.yml loads docker/weather.env via `env_file:` on
# weather-tracker only — weather-web never receives provider credentials,
# since its HTTP-facing process has no use for them; it gets its own
# explicit, narrower `environment:` in docker-compose.yml instead. A few
# of these same variables are also read directly for host-port/bind
# interpolation (documented below).
#
# Every variable below is a placeholder — no real API keys, no real
# locations.

# --- Secrets -----------------------------------------------------------

# OpenWeather API key used by the tracker to poll current conditions.
# Placeholder only — get a real key from https://openweathermap.org/api.
CLIMATE_OPENWEATHER_API_KEY=REPLACE_WITH_OPENWEATHER_KEY

# Israel Meteorological Service (IMS) API token, used by the tracker for
# IMS-sourced observations. Placeholder only.
CLIMATE_IMS_API_TOKEN=REPLACE_WITH_IMS_TOKEN

# --- Storage -------------------------------------------------------------

# Mongo connection string used by both tracker and web. Points at the
# compose-network service name, NOT localhost — weather-mongodb publishes
# no host port by default, so this is only reachable from other containers
# on the same compose network.
WEATHER_MONGO_URI=mongodb://weather-mongodb:27017/weather

# --- Web service bind/port ------------------------------------------------

# Host interface the web service's HTTP port is published on. Defaults to
# 127.0.0.1 (loopback-only) in docker-compose.yml if unset here.
CLIMATE_WEB_BIND=127.0.0.1

# Host port the web service is published on (container always listens on
# 8095 internally — matches the fixed default base URL
# http://127.0.0.1:8095 in the merged API contract, docs/weather-api.md).
# Defaults to 8095 in docker-compose.yml if unset here.
CLIMATE_WEB_PORT=8095

# --- Config mount ---------------------------------------------------------

# Host directory containing the user's weather config (locations, alert
# thresholds, etc). This is private data and must live OUTSIDE the repo.
# Mounted read-only into both the tracker and web containers at
# /app/config. Defaults to ${HOME}/.config/climate-cli in
# docker-compose.yml if unset here — matching the config task's own
# default resolution of $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/climate-cli
# (~/.config/climate-cli/weather.json), overridable there via
# CLIMATE_WEATHER_CONFIG_PATH.
CLIMATE_WEATHER_CONFIG_DIR=/home/REPLACE_WITH_USER/.config/climate-cli

# NOTE: CLIMATE_WEATHER_CONFIG_PATH (the in-container path the tracker/web
# processes read the config file from, /app/config/weather.json) is set
# directly in docker-compose.yml's `environment:` for both services — it
# is fixed to the mount point above plus the filename the config task's
# resolver expects, so it is not a variable to set here.

# --- Optional debug mongo publish -----------------------------------------

# By default, weather-mongodb is NOT reachable from the host at all. To
# temporarily expose it on host port 27020 for local debugging (e.g. a
# GUI mongo client), apply the debug override file on top of the base one
# instead of setting anything here:
#   docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.debug.yml up -d weather-mongodb
# That override adds only a port publish to the existing weather-mongodb
# service — it must never start a second mongod process against the same
# volume. There is no env var to flip for this on purpose — the override
# file keeps the debug publish an explicit, opt-in command rather than a
# config value that could be left on accidentally.
